\section*{Problem 1}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Problem 1}

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(1) Competitive market}

In a competitive market, both buyers and sellers are \textbf{price takers}: they accept the market price $p^*$ as given and cannot influence it. The \textbf{market clearing (equilibrium) price} $p^*$ and \textbf{market clearing quantity} $q^*$ are determined by the intersection of the \textbf{demand curve} $D(p)$ and the \textbf{supply curve} $S(p)$:

\[
D(p^*) = S(p^*) = q^* .
\]

At any price above $p^*$, supply exceeds demand (surplus); at any price below $p^*$, demand exceeds supply (shortage). Only at $p^*$ do the two forces balance.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth, scale=1.1]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$q$};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above] {$p$};
  \node[left] at (0,5) {$p$};
  \node[below] at (5.5,0) {$q$};

  % Supply
  \draw[thick, blue, domain=0.5:5] plot (\x, {0.5+\x}) node[right] {$S(p)$};
  % Demand
  \draw[thick, red, domain=0:4.5] plot (\x, {5-1*\x}) node[right] {$D(p)$};

  % Intersection
  \coordinate (eq) at (3,3.5);
  \draw[dashed] (eq) -- (0,3.5) node[left] {$p^*$};
  \draw[dashed] (eq) -- (3,0) node[below] {$q^*$};

  \fill (eq) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(q^*,p^*)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Intuition:} The equilibrium is \textbf{stable} because any deviation from $p^*$ creates a force that pushes the market back. If $p > p^*$, firms cannot sell all they produce, so they lower price; if $p < p^*$, goods sell out quickly, and consumers bid the price up.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(2) Monopoly (single seller)}

Here a \textbf{single seller} (monopolist) faces many buyers who are price takers. The monopolist chooses quantity $q$ (or equivalently, price $p$ via the inverse demand curve $p = D^{-1}(q)$) to \textbf{maximize profit}:

\[
\max_{q \geq 0} \quad \pi(q) = p(q) \cdot q - C(q),
\]

where $p(q) = D^{-1}(q)$ is the inverse demand and $C(q)$ is the total cost. The \textbf{first-order condition} (FOC) gives:

\[
\frac{d\pi}{dq} = 0 \quad \Longrightarrow \quad MR(q) = MC(q),
\]

where $MR(q) = p(q) + q \cdot p'(q)$ is the \textbf{marginal revenue} and $MC(q) = C'(q)$ is the \textbf{marginal cost}. The \textbf{market clearing price} $p^*$ is then read off the demand curve at the optimal quantity $q^*$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth, scale=1.1]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {$q$};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above] {$p$};

  % Demand
  \draw[thick, red, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {5-0.8*\x}) node[right] {$D(p)$};
  % MR (steeper than D for linear case)
  \draw[thick, red!60!black, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {5-1.6*\x}) node[right] {$MR(q)$};
  % MC
  \draw[thick, blue, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {1+0.3*\x}) node[right] {$MC(q)$};

  % Monopoly point
  \coordinate (m) at (2.86,2.71);
  \coordinate (mc\_m) at (2.86,1.86);
  \coordinate (d\_m) at (2.86,2.71);
  \draw[dashed] (m) -- (0,2.71) node[left] {$p^m$};
  \draw[dashed] (m) -- (2.86,0) node[below] {$q^m$};

  % Competitive point
  \coordinate (c) at (4,1);
  \draw[dashed] (c) -- (0,1) node[left] {$p^c$};
  \draw[dashed] (c) -- (4,0) node[below] {$q^c$};

  \fill (m) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(q^m,p^m)$};
  \fill (c) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(q^c,p^c)$};

  % Deadweight loss
  \draw[thick, dashed, orange] (m) -- (c);
  \node[orange, right] at (3.4,1.8) {DWL};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Key insight:} The monopolist produces $q^m < q^c$ and charges $p^m > p^c$. Because $MR$ is always \textbf{below} the demand curve (each extra unit sold lowers the price on all units), the monopoly quantity is \textbf{inefficient}. The shaded triangle between $q^m$ and $q^c$ is the \textbf{deadweight loss} (DWL) of monopoly.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(3) Monopsony (single buyer)}

A \textbf{single buyer} (monopsonist) faces many sellers (price takers). The monopsonist sets the \textbf{quantity purchased} $q$ (equivalently, the price $p$ it pays per unit). Let the aggregate supply curve be $p = S(q)$, where $S(q)$ is the minimum price at which sellers are willing to supply quantity $q$.

The monopsonist's total cost of buying $q$ units is $C(q) = p(q) \cdot q = S(q) \cdot q$. Suppose the monopsonist has a revenue function $R(q)$ from the output it produces (or a benefit function $B(q)$). To purchase $q$ units, the cost is $C(q)$, so the profit maximization problem is:

\[
\max_{q \geq 0} \quad \pi(q) = B(q) - C(q)
     = B(q) - S(q) \cdot q .
\]

\textbf{First-order condition:}

\[
\frac{d\pi}{dq} = 0
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad
\underbrace{B'(q)}_{MB} = \underbrace{\frac{d}{dq}\bigl[S(q) \cdot q\bigr]}_{MC_{\text{buy}}}
= S(q) + q \cdot S'(q) .
\]

The left side is the \textbf{marginal benefit} (or marginal revenue product, $MRP$) of buying one more unit; the right side is the \textbf{marginal expenditure} (cost) of buying one more unit. This is the analogue of $MR = MC$ for a buyer.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth, scale=1.1]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {$q$};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above] {$p$};

  % Supply
  \draw[thick, blue, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {0.5+0.7*\x}) node[right] {$S(p)$};
  % MB / demand
  \draw[thick, red, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {5-0.8*\x}) node[right] {$MB(q)$};
  % MEC (steeper than S for upward-sloping supply)
  \draw[thick, red!60!black, domain=0:5] plot (\x, {0.5+1.4*\x}) node[right] {$MEC(q)$};

  % Monopsony point
  \coordinate (mono) at (2.14,2);
  \draw[dashed] (mono) -- (0,2) node[left] {$p^m$};
  \draw[dashed] (mono) -- (2.14,0) node[below] {$q^m$};

  % Competitive point
  \coordinate (c) at (4,1);
  \draw[dashed] (c) -- (0,1) node[left] {$p^c$};
  \draw[dashed] (c) -- (4,0) node[below] {$q^c$};

  \fill (mono) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(q^m,p^m)$};
  \fill (c) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(q^c,p^c)$};

  % Deadweight loss
  \draw[thick, dashed, orange] (mono) -- (c);
  \node[orange, right] at (3.0,1.5) {DWL};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Intuition:} The monopsonist faces an \textbf{upward-sloping supply curve} of the input. To buy one more unit, it must raise the price \textbf{for all units} (because all sellers receive the same price). Hence the \textbf{marginal expenditure} ($MEC$) lies \textbf{above} the supply curve. The monopsonist buys $q^m < q^c$ at $p^m < p^c$. The DWL triangle again captures the efficiency loss from market power.

\textbf{Key comparison:}

\begin{tabular}{lccc}
  \hline
  & Competitive & Monopoly & Monopsony \\
  \hline
  Price paid by buyer & $p^c$ & $p^m > p^c$ & $p^m < p^c$ \\
  Quantity traded & $q^c$ & $q^m < q^c$ & $q^m < q^c$ \\
  Efficiency benchmark & --- & DWL = triangle & DWL = triangle \\
  \hline
\end{tabular}

