\section*{Problem 2}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Problem 2}

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{Given data}

\textbf{Generator cost functions:}

\[
c_A(p_A) = 20 + 1.7\, p_A + 0.04\, p_A^2,
\qquad
c_B(p_B) = 16 + 1.8\, p_B + 0.03\, p_B^2 .
\]

\textbf{Marginal cost functions (derivatives):}

\[
\begin{aligned}
MC_A(p_A) &= \frac{d c_A}{d p_A} = 1.7 + 0.08\, p_A, \\[4pt]
MC_B(p_B) &= \frac{d c_B}{d p_B} = 1.8 + 0.06\, p_B .
\end{aligned}
\]

\textbf{Electricity pool offers (from consumers) --- see Figure~\ref{fig:bids}.}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{offers_table.png}
  \caption{Outstanding offers from consumers in the electricity pool market.}
  \label{fig:bids}
\end{figure}

From the bids/offers table, the market clearing price is determined where the total generation capacity offered at or below that price meets total demand. The \textbf{market clearing price} $p_{\text{MCP}}$ is read from the offer curve.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(1) Optimal dispatch and profit under offer O1}

Accepting offer O1 means the company commits to supply a certain quantity at the offer price. The company dispatches its cheaper generators first (lowest marginal cost) until the offered quantity is met. Rank the marginal costs at each generator level:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
  \hline
  Unit & Output (MW) & $MC$ (\$/MWh) \\
  \hline
  $B$ & $p_B = 0$   & $MC_B(0) = 1.80$ \\
  $B$ & $p_B = 100$ & $MC_B(100) = 1.8 + 0.06(100) = 7.80$ \\
  $B$ & $p_B = 200$ & $MC_B(200) = 1.8 + 0.06(200) = 13.80$ \\
  $B$ & $p_B = 300$ & $MC_B(300) = 1.8 + 0.06(300) = 19.80$ \\
  $A$ & $p_A = 0$   & $MC_A(0) = 1.70$ \\
  $A$ & $p_A = 100$ & $MC_A(100) = 1.7 + 0.08(100) = 9.70$ \\
  $A$ & $p_A = 200$ & $MC_A(200) = 1.7 + 0.08(200) = 17.70$ \\
  \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Sorted by marginal cost (lowest first):
\[
\underbrace{MC_B(0)\to 300}_{B:\;0\text{--}300\text{ MW}} \;
<\;
\underbrace{MC_A(0)\to 100}_{A:\;0\text{--}100\text{ MW}} \;
<\;
\underbrace{MC_B(100)\to 200}_{B:\;100\text{--}300\text{ MW}} .
\]

\textbf{Optimal dispatch under O1:} Dispatch all of unit $B$ (300 MW) and 100 MW of unit $A$:
\[
p_A^* = 100\text{ MW}, \qquad p_B^* = 300\text{ MW}, \qquad q^* = 400\text{ MW}.
\]

\textbf{Revenue:} $R = p_{\text{MCP}} \times q^*$.

\textbf{Total cost:}
\[
\begin{aligned}
C &= c_A(100) + c_B(300) \\
  &= \bigl[20 + 1.7(100) + 0.04(100)^2\bigr]
   + \bigl[16 + 1.8(300) + 0.03(300)^2\bigr] \\
  &= [20 + 170 + 400] + [16 + 540 + 2700] \\
  &= 590 + 3256 = 3846 .
\end{aligned}
\]

\textbf{Profit:}
\[
\pi = R - C = p_{\text{MCP}} \times 400 - 3846 .
\]

(Substitute the market clearing price from the offer table to obtain the numerical value.)

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(2) Should the company also accept offer O2?}

Accepting O2 commits the company to supply an additional quantity $q_2$ at a lower (or the same) offer price. The key question is whether this additional dispatch is \textbf{profitable}, i.e., whether the marginal cost of the extra generation is below the offer price.

If the offer price of O2 is $p_{\text{O2}}$, the company should accept O2 if and only if:

\[
\text{MC of the next cheapest MW} \leq p_{\text{O2}} .
\]

After accepting O1, the generation stack is already:
\[
B\text{ (first 300 MW)} \longrightarrow A\text{ (100 MW)}.
\]

The next cheapest MW comes from \textbf{unit A's second block} ($p_A = 100\text{--}200$ MW) or \textbf{unit B's second block} ($p_B = 300\text{--}400$ MW), whichever is cheaper at the margin. Compare $MC_A(200)$ and $MC_B(400)$ with $p_{\text{O2}}$:

\[
MC_A(200) = 1.7 + 0.08(200) = 17.70\ \text{\$/MWh},
\qquad
MC_B(400) = 1.8 + 0.06(400) = 25.80\ \text{\$/MWh}.
\]

\textbf{Rule:} Accept O2 if $p_{\text{O2}} \geq \min\{MC_A(200),\; MC_B(400)\} = 17.70$.

If $p_{\text{O2}} \geq 17.70$, dispatch the next-cheapest block; if $p_{\text{O2}} < 17.70$, reject O2 (dispatching any remaining unit would lose money).

\textbf{Intuition:} The generating company should only accept an offer if the marginal cost of supplying the extra electricity does not exceed the price offered. The profit-maximizing dispatch always follows the \textbf{merit order} --- generators with the lowest marginal cost are dispatched first. Once all generators with $MC \leq p_{\text{MCP}}$ are fully dispatched, no further offers should be accepted.

\textbf{Conclusion:} Accept offer O2 only if $p_{\text{O2}} \geq 17.70$~\$/MWh. Otherwise, the marginal cost of additional generation exceeds the offer price, and accepting O2 would reduce the company's profit.

