\section*{Problem 3}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Problem 3}

\subsection*{Model setup}

With both a per-trip congestion charge $p_3$ and a minimum driver wage $w$, the TNC platform solves:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\max_{p_1,\; p_2} \;& \lambda\,(p_1 - p_2) \\
\text{s.t.} \quad &
\lambda = F_p\!\left(\frac{\alpha M}{\sqrt{N}} - \frac{\lambda}{\mu} + p_1 + p_3\right), \quad
\text{(passenger side)} \\[4pt]
& N \leq F_d\!\left(\frac{\lambda\, p_2}{N}\right), \quad
\text{(driver supply)} \\[4pt]
& \frac{\lambda\, p_2}{N} \geq w . \quad
\text{(minimum wage constraint)}
\end{aligned}
\]

Here:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $p_1$: price charged to passengers (fare component).
  \item $p_2$: wage component paid to drivers per trip (net of platform's take).
  \item $\lambda$: rate of matched trips (passenger arrival rate).
  \item $N$: number of active drivers in the platform pool.
  \item $p_3$: congestion charge per trip (fixed in this problem).
  \item $w$: minimum wage floor (to be perturbed).
\end{itemize}

The supply side determines $N$ as the minimum of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The driver distribution evaluated at the average driver earning $\lambda p_2 / N$:
    $N_{\max}^{(1)} = F_d(\lambda p_2 / N)$.
  \item The constraint from the minimum wage floor:
    $N \leq \lambda p_2 / w$.
\end{enumerate}

Define the average driver earnings as $\omega \equiv \lambda p_2 / N$.
The binding constraint is:
\[
N \leq \min\!\bigl\{F_d(\omega),\; \lambda p_2 / w \bigr\}.
\]

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{Three regimes of $N$ vs.\ $w$}

Fix $p_3$ and vary $w$. The equilibrium $N$ follows a \textbf{piecewise} relationship with three regimes:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Stealth, scale=1.2]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (7,0) node[right] {$w$ (minimum wage)};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$N$};

  % Regime 1: flat
  \draw[thick, blue, domain=0:2] plot (\x, {4}) node[right] {};
  \node[below] at (1,0) {$0$};

  % Regime 2: upward slope
  \draw[thick, red, domain=2:4.5] plot (\x, {0.8+0.8*(\x-2)}) node[right] {};

  % Regime 3: downward slope
  \draw[thick, green!60!black, domain=4.5:6.5] plot (\x, {4.8-0.9*(\x-4.5)}) node[right] {};

  % Connection dots
  \fill (2,4) circle (1.5pt);
  \fill (4.5,4) circle (1.5pt);

  % Regime labels
  \node[below, blue] at (1,0.3) {Regime 1};
  \node[below, red] at (3.2,0.3) {Regime 2};
  \node[below, green!60!black] at (5.5,0.3) {Regime 3};

  % Reference lines
  \draw[dashed] (2,0) -- (2,4);
  \draw[dashed] (4.5,0) -- (4.5,4);
  \draw[dashed] (0,4) -- (4.5,4) node[left] {$N^*$};

  % Critical wage labels
  \node[below] at (2,0) {$w^*$};
  \node[below] at (4.5,0) {$w_1$};

  % Arrows on curves
  \draw[thick, blue, ->] (1.5,4) -- (1.9,4);
  \draw[thick, red, ->] (2.2,4.16) -- (2.6,4.48);
  \draw[thick, red, ->] (3.8,4.64) -- (4.3,4.8);
  \draw[thick, green!60!black, ->] (4.7,4.72) -- (5.1,4.36);
  \draw[thick, green!60!black, ->] (5.8,4) -- (6.2,3.64);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\noindent\textbf{Regime 1: $w < w^*$ (below optimal wage without regulation)}

The minimum wage is \textbf{not binding}. The platform chooses $w$ and $N$ freely to satisfy $MR = MC$. The equilibrium $N^*$ and $\omega^*$ are the same as the unconstrained optimum. Since $w$ is below the wage the platform would voluntarily pay, the minimum wage has no effect:

\[
N(w) = N^*, \qquad \frac{dN}{dw} = 0 \quad (w < w^*).
\]

\bigskip
\noindent\textbf{Regime 2: $w^* \leq w \leq w_1$ (middle range)}

The minimum wage is binding ($\omega \geq w$) but the \textbf{driver supply constraint} $N \leq F_d(\omega)$ is \textbf{not} yet binding. Because $w$ is now an effective floor on driver earnings, \textbf{more drivers are willing to join} the platform (the supply constraint is relaxed). The platform expands service and hires more drivers:

\[
\frac{dN}{dw} > 0 .
\]

Intuitively, raising the minimum wage attracts more drivers, which increases the matching rate $\lambda$, which in turn attracts more passengers (two-sided market amplification). This is the \textbf{counterintuitive} result: minimum wage \emph{increases} employment.

From the lecture simulation results, in this regime:
\begin{itemize}
  \item More drivers are hired ($N$ increases).
  \item Each driver earns the higher wage $w$.
  \item More passengers are served ($\lambda$ increases).
  \item Each passenger pays a smaller fare (per-trip cost decreases due to better matching).
  \item TNC platform profit decreases.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\noindent\textbf{Regime 3: $w > w_1$ (high minimum wage)}

Both the minimum wage constraint \textbf{and} the driver supply constraint $N \leq F_d(\omega)$ are binding. Raising $w$ further forces the platform to pay even more per driver, but now the driver pool is constrained by the distribution $F_d$. The platform reduces driver hiring to control cost, and the equilibrium $N$ falls:

\[
\frac{dN}{dw} < 0 .
\]

This is the \textbf{classical} monopsony response: a higher wage floor raises marginal cost, the platform cuts hiring, and service shrinks. This matches the standard intuition from competitive markets where minimum wage reduces employment.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{Summary: $N$ vs.\ $w$}

\[
N(w) =
\begin{cases}
  N^*,                            & w < w^*, \\[4pt]
  \text{increasing function of } w, & w^* \leq w \leq w_1, \\[4pt]
  \text{decreasing function of } w, & w > w_1 .
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
  \hline
  Regime & Wage range & $dN/dw$ & Binding constraint \\
  \hline
  1 & $w < w^*$ & $= 0$ & None (unconstrained optimum) \\
  2 & $w^* \leq w \leq w_1$ & $> 0$ & Minimum wage only \\
  3 & $w > w_1$ & $< 0$ & Minimum wage + driver supply \\
  \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

This piecewise relationship is the mirror image of the $N$ vs.\ $p_3$ analysis from Lecture 21 (where $p_3$ was perturbed with $w$ fixed). Here, perturbing $w$ with $p_3$ fixed traces the same three-regime structure but in the opposite direction along the supply curve.

