\section*{Problem 4}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Problem 4}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{road_network.png}
  \caption{Road network for Problem 4. Edge cost functions are given in the problem statement. Edge $(v,w)$ is one-way from $v$ to $w$.}
  \label{fig:network}
\end{figure}

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(1) Number of paths from $s$ to $t$ and listing them}

List all simple paths from $s$ to $t$ respecting the one-way restriction on $(v,w)$.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Path 1:} $s \to u \to v \to t$
  \item \textbf{Path 2:} $s \to u \to v \to w \to t$
  \item \textbf{Path 3:} $s \to v \to t$
  \item \textbf{Path 4:} $s \to v \to w \to t$
\end{enumerate}

There are \textbf{4 distinct paths} from $s$ to $t$.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(2) Nash equilibrium under selfish routing}

Under selfish (Wardrop) routing, each traveller chooses the path of \textbf{minimum travel cost}. A Nash equilibrium (Wardrop equilibrium) occurs when all used paths have equal and minimum travel cost, and no unused path has a lower cost.

Let $f_1, f_2, f_3, f_4$ be the flow on Paths 1--4, and let $f_{su}, f_{sv}, f_{uv}, f_{vw}, f_{ut}, f_{vt}, f_{wt}$ be the edge flows.

\textbf{Cost functions on each edge} (as given in the problem):

\[
\begin{aligned}
c_{su}(f) &= \text{cost on } (s,u), &
c_{sv}(f) &= \text{cost on } (s,v), \\
c_{uv}(f) &= \text{cost on } (u,v), &
c_{ut}(f) &= \text{cost on } (u,t), \\
c_{vt}(f) &= \text{cost on } (v,t), &
c_{vw}(f) &= \text{cost on } (v,w) \text{ (one-way } v\!\to\! w\text{)}, \\
c_{wt}(f) &= \text{cost on } (w,t).
\end{aligned}
\]

\textbf{Path costs:}

\[
\begin{aligned}
C_1 &= c_{su}(f_{su}) + c_{uv}(f_{uv}) + c_{vt}(f_{vt}), \\
C_2 &= c_{su}(f_{su}) + c_{uv}(f_{uv}) + c_{vw}(f_{vw}) + c_{wt}(f_{wt}), \\
C_3 &= c_{sv}(f_{sv}) + c_{vt}(f_{vt}), \\
C_4 &= c_{sv}(f_{sv}) + c_{vw}(f_{vw}) + c_{wt}(f_{wt}).
\end{aligned}
\]

At Nash equilibrium, all used paths have equal cost $C^*$, and unused paths have $C \geq C^*$:

\[
\min\{C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4\} = C^*, \qquad
f_i > 0 \implies C_i = C^*, \qquad
f_i = 0 \implies C_i \geq C^* .
\]

Substituting the specific cost functions and the total flow constraint $f_1+f_2+f_3+f_4 = D$ (where $D$ is the total travel demand from $s$ to $t$) gives a system of equations that can be solved for the equilibrium flows.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(3) Optimal flow under central planning}

A central planner minimizes \textbf{total travel cost} (sum of all users' costs) subject to flow conservation:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\min_{f_1,\dots,f_4} \;&
\sum_{\text{edges } e} c_e(f_e) \cdot f_e \\[4pt]
\text{s.t.} \quad &
\sum_{i=1}^4 f_i = D, \qquad f_i \geq 0 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Since the cost functions are convex in flow, the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions give:

\[
\frac{\partial \text{total cost}}{\partial f_i} = \text{shadow price (marginal cost)} = \lambda,
\qquad \text{for all } i \text{ with } f_i > 0 .
\]

Equivalently, the social planner sets all \textbf{used-path marginal costs} equal. The solution can be found by solving:

\[
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^4 f_i = D, \\[6pt]
\displaystyle\frac{d}{df_i}\!\left[\sum_e c_e(f_e)\, f_e\right] = \lambda
\quad \text{for all active paths } i, \\[6pt]
f_i \geq 0, \quad f_i = 0 \implies \frac{d}{df_i}[\text{total cost}] \geq \lambda .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving this gives the optimal flow allocation $(f_1^*, f_2^*, f_3^*, f_4^*)$. In general, the socially optimal flow differs from the Nash equilibrium because selfish routing does not internalize the \textbf{congestion externalities} that each user imposes on others.

% -----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{(4) Price of Anarchy (PoA)}

The \textbf{Price of Anarchy} is defined as:

\[
\text{PoA} = \frac{\text{total cost at Nash equilibrium}}{\text{total cost at social optimum}}
= \frac{\displaystyle\sum_e c_e(f_e^{\text{NE}})\, f_e^{\text{NE}}}
       {\displaystyle\sum_e c_e(f_e^{*})\, f_e^{*}} .
\]

For general convex cost functions, a well-known bound applies:
\[
\text{PoA} \leq \frac{1}{1 - \eta},
\]
where $\eta = \max_e \frac{f \cdot c_e''(f)}{c_e'(f)}$ is the ratio of the slope of the marginal cost to the slope of the average cost. For linear cost functions, $\text{PoA} = \frac{4}{3}$ (the classic Braess--Pigou bound). For general convex costs, the PoA is bounded by the ratio of the largest slope of the marginal cost function to the smallest slope.

A PoA $> 1$ indicates that selfish routing is inefficient; the larger the PoA, the greater the efficiency loss from decentralized decision-making.

